<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  Vespa application for the cycling corpus.

  One container cluster and two content clusters, all on a single node, which is
  what a laptop Docker run can hold. The two content clusters hold the same
  passages in the two storage modes Vespa offers - indexed and streaming - so
  the page can run one question through both and show what the difference costs.
  The other interesting part is the pair of embedder components.

  The embedders matter because they are what let the browser page be a thin
  client. The question arrives as plain text and Vespa turns it into vectors
  itself, at query time, with the same models it used at feed time. Without them
  the page would have to carry an embedding model, which is exactly the job the
  in-browser version of this project does and exactly the job this version is
  meant to hand to a server.
-->
<services version="1.0">

  <container id="cycling" version="1.0">

    <document-api/>
    <search/>
    <!-- Index-time embedding runs in the indexing document-processing chain,
         which lives in a container. Feeding fails without this element. -->
    <document-processing/>

    <!--
      e5-small-v2, the same model the in-browser version of this project uses,
      so the two are comparable. e5 is asymmetric: it was trained with "query:"
      on questions and "passage:" on documents and loses most of its accuracy
      without them. Here Vespa applies both prefixes itself, which removes the
      commonest way to get this wrong - prefixing at feed time and forgetting at
      query time, which degrades quietly rather than failing.
    -->
    <component id="e5" type="hugging-face-embedder">
      <transformer-model url="https://huggingface.co/intfloat/e5-small-v2/resolve/main/model.onnx"/>
      <tokenizer-model url="https://huggingface.co/intfloat/e5-small-v2/raw/main/tokenizer.json"/>
      <prepend>
        <query>query:</query>
        <document>passage:</document>
      </prepend>
    </component>

    <!--
      ColBERT produces one vector per token instead of one per passage, so a
      budget has to be set for both sides. 32 query tokens is more than any
      question typed into the box. 256 document tokens covers most of a
      900-character passage but not all of one: anything past the limit is
      simply not represented, which is a real limitation of this profile and is
      written up as such on the page rather than hidden.
    -->
    <component id="colbert" type="colbert-embedder">
      <transformer-model url="https://huggingface.co/colbert-ir/colbertv2.0/resolve/main/model.onnx"/>
      <tokenizer-model url="https://huggingface.co/colbert-ir/colbertv2.0/raw/main/tokenizer.json"/>
      <max-query-tokens>32</max-query-tokens>
      <max-document-tokens>256</max-document-tokens>
    </component>

    <!--
      No <http> block, so Vespa creates its default server on 8080.

      There was a CORS filter chain here, and it is the reason the first deploy
      of this package never converged:

        WARNING Unable to find config definition
                'jdisc.http.filter.security.cors.cors-filter.def'

      The config server validates every <config name="..."> against a config
      definition it knows about, and the CORS definition is not among the ones
      shipped in share/vespa/configdefinitions. Deploy then hangs rather than
      failing, which is a slow way to learn this.

      It is not needed for local use anyway. The dev server proxies /search/
      through to :8080 (see the "proxy" field in package.json), so the page and
      the engine are the same origin as far as the browser is concerned and no
      preflight happens. That is both simpler and more robust than configuring
      CORS correctly.

      CORS only becomes necessary for a page served from another origin - a
      deployed site on gillesvandevyver.com calling a cluster on your laptop.
      If you want that, the documented mechanism for filters that live in JDisc
      bundles is <filter-config>, not <config>:

        <http>
          <filtering strict-mode="false">
            <request-chain id="cors-request-chain">
              <filter id="com.yahoo.jdisc.http.filter.security.cors.CorsPreflightRequestFilter"
                      bundle="jdisc-security-filters">
                <filter-config>
                  <corsAllowedUrls>https://gillesvandevyver.com</corsAllowedUrls>
                </filter-config>
              </filter>
              <binding>http://*/search/</binding>
            </request-chain>
            ...
          </filtering>
          <server port="8080" id="default"/>
        </http>

      That form is untested here - verify it against a running container before
      trusting it, and check the key name with:

        docker exec vespa-cycling \
          ls /opt/vespa/share/vespa/configdefinitions/ | grep -i cors
    -->

    <nodes>
      <node hostalias="node1"/>
    </nodes>

  </container>

  <!--
    Indexed mode: the whole corpus, searched as one collection.

    Everything is built up front - the HNSW graph over the passage vectors, the
    BM25 posting lists, the in-memory attributes - and a query touches a small
    part of it. This is what you want when any query may match any document.
  -->
  <content id="cycling-content" version="1.0">
    <!-- One copy of each document. Fine for a demo on one node; a real cluster
         would want at least 2 so that losing a node does not lose data. -->
    <redundancy>1</redundancy>
    <documents>
      <document type="passage" mode="index"/>
    </documents>
    <nodes>
      <node hostalias="node1" distribution-key="0"/>
    </nodes>
  </content>

  <!--
    Streaming mode: the same passages, but partitioned by article, and searched
    one article at a time.

    The two clusters hold the same text on purpose. Streaming mode is usually
    explained with mailboxes - every query belongs to exactly one user, so there
    is no point indexing across users - and a mailbox is hard to put on a public
    demo page. An article is the same shape of thing: a group you always search
    the whole of, and never across.

    What changes is what gets built. No inverted index, no HNSW graph; the
    documents in the selected group are read and matched one by one, and the
    nearest-neighbour search over them is exact rather than approximate. That
    costs more per query inside a group and saves nearly everything per
    document, which is the right trade when the groups are small and numerous:
    a million mailboxes cost a million mailboxes' worth of disk and no index at
    all. The equivalent indexed cluster is the one above, and the page lets you
    run the same question through both.

    The separate document type is forced rather than chosen: mode is a property
    of a document type in a content cluster, so one type cannot be both.
  -->
  <content id="cycling-streaming" version="1.0">
    <redundancy>1</redundancy>
    <documents>
      <document type="article_passage" mode="streaming"/>
    </documents>
    <nodes>
      <node hostalias="node1" distribution-key="0"/>
    </nodes>
  </content>

</services>
